The question asks whether it's true that a private method of a parent class is not part of the inheritance contract, so a child class can declare a method with the same name with any visibility.

In PHP, when a parent class has a private method, that method is NOT part of the inheritance contract. The child class can indeed declare a method with the same name with any visibility (public, protected, private) without conflict. This is because private members are not inherited in the traditional sense — they are hidden from the child class.

This is correct. For example:

```php
class A {
    private function foo() { return 1; }
}

class B extends A {
    public function foo() { return 2; }
}
```

This works fine. The child's `foo()` is a completely separate method from the parent's `foo()`.

So the answer is "да" (true).

