The question asks whether an abstract method in an abstract class can have protected visibility.

In PHP, abstract methods in abstract classes can have public or protected visibility. They cannot be private (that would be a fatal error since a private method can't be overridden in a child class). So yes, an abstract method can be protected.

Example:
```php
abstract class Foo {
    abstract protected function bar();
}
```

This is valid PHP. So the answer is "да" (yes, it's correct).
